WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.232
At 3 a.m.

2
00:00:01.252 --> 00:00:02.570
your container dies.

3
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.205
With Docker alone, it stays dead.

4
00:00:05.225 --> 00:00:07.243
Docker runs what you tell it, once.

5
00:00:07.263 --> 00:00:09.215
Nobody watches it for you.

6
00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:14.415
This port has three ideas: the control loop, the pod, and the manifest.

7
00:00:14.435 --> 00:00:17.775
First: the one idea behind all of Kubernetes.

8
00:00:17.795 --> 00:00:19.125
You declare what you want.

9
00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:21.835
For example: three copies of my app.

10
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.485
A controller observes, compares, and acts.

11
00:00:24.505 --> 00:00:27.360
Then it does that again, without end.

12
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.720
Second: what does Kubernetes run?

13
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.020
It runs pods.

14
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.380
A pod is one or more containers that share one address.

15
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.443
Most pods hold one container.

16
00:00:36.463 --> 00:00:39.505
Think of a pod as a wrapper around it.

17
00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:41.315
Third: who heals what?

18
00:00:41.335 --> 00:00:45.775
If a container crashes, the node restarts it inside the same pod.

19
00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.762
But if you delete a bare pod, nothing brings it back.

20
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:51.575
That is why controllers exist.

21
00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:53.760
Last: how do you say what you want?

22
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:54.910
You write a file.

23
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.835
You fill in the spec, and the cluster fills in the status.

24
00:00:58.855 --> 00:01:01.145
Quick recap of the fundamentals.

25
00:01:01.165 --> 00:01:02.758
Declare, do not command.

26
00:01:02.778 --> 00:01:04.495
A pod wraps containers.

27
00:01:04.515 --> 00:01:06.680
And a bare pod does not come back.

28
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.800
Now start the first lesson below.
